hem","ph":"+46 31 13 49 60","ad":"6 Majorsgatan","f":["card"]},{"n":"Rondo kemtvätt","g":"essentials","k":"dry_cleaning","la":57.699304,"lo":11.936367,"d":831},{"n":"Pedagogen, hus A","g":"essentials","k":"wifi","la":57.702002,"lo":11.964236,"d":853,"ad":"25 Västra Hamngatan","f":["wc","card","ac"],"wifi":1},{"n":"Folkteatern","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.700556,"lo":11.953333,"d":204,"ds":"Folkteatern is a regional theatre in Gothenburg, Sweden. The theater receives grants and assignments from the Västra Götaland Regional Council. The theatre was built in 1951 at Järntorget and has from the start had close connections with the worker's movement.","wpu":"Folkteatern","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Folkteaterns_entr%C3%A9.JPG/960px-Folkteaterns_entr%C3%A9.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Järntorget, Gothenburg","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.699722,"lo":11.952778,"d":217,"ds":"Järntorget is a public square in Gothenburg, Sweden. A hub for public transport (see Gothenburg tram system), it is located west of Haga and is the starting point for the boulevards of Linnégatan to the south and Nya Allén to the east; and the streets of Första Långgatan and Andra Långgatan to the west.","wpu":"Järntorget, Gothenburg","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/J%C3%A4rntorget%2C_den_1_juli_2006%2C_bild_1.JPG/960px-J%C3%A4rntorget%2C_den_1_juli_2006%2C_bild_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Feskekôrka","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.701111,"lo":11.957778,"d":462,"ds":"Feskekörka (Swedish: [ˈfɛ̂sːkɛˌɕœrːka]; standard: Fiskkyrkan, lit. 'fish church', also Feskekôrka) is a building in Gothenburg, Sweden, that formerly housed an indoor fish market. Its name is based on the building's resemblance to a neo-Gothic church. It opened on 1 November 1874, and was designed by the city architect Victor von Gegerfelt.","wpu":"Feskekôrka","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Feskek%C3%B4rka%2C_2019_%2804%29.jpg/960px-Feskek%C3%B4rka%2C_2019_%2804%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Oscar Fredrik Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.696667,"lo":11.947222,"d":509,"ds":"Oscar Fredrik Church (Swedish: Oscar Fredriks kyrka) is a church in Olivedal in Gothenburg, Sweden. It was drawn by Helgo Zetterwall and erected in the 1890s. Belonging to the Gothenburg Oscar Fredrik Parish of the Church of Sweden (Swedish: Svenska Kyrkan), it was inaugurated on Easter Sunday, Sunday, 2 April 1893.","wpu":"Oscar Fredrik Church","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Oscar_Fredriks_kyrka_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Skansen Kronan","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.696667,"lo":11.954722,"d":557,"ds":"Skansen Kronan (\"the Crown Sconce\") is a redoubt on the hill Risåsberget, in the Haga district of Gothenburg, Sweden. The fortress and its twin, Skansen Lejonet, were built to protect the city of Gothenburg against possible Danish attack, and thus had a similar purpose to the sea fort of New Älvsborg, which was built around the same time.","wpu":"Skansen Kronan","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Skansen_Kronan_Gbg.jpg/960px-Skansen_Kronan_Gbg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Raoul Wallenberg Monument, Gothenburg","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.69841,"lo":11.96164,"d":749,"ds":"The Raoul Wallenberg Monument, titled In Memory of Raoul Wallenberg's Deed, was erected near the Haga Church (Hagakyrkan) in Gothenburg, Sweden. It is a 2.45 meter high monument, made partly of graphic concrete and partly of bronze, and it was created by Charlotte Gyllenhammar of Stockholm, Sweden. It was unveiled on May 25, 2007 by the UN Secretary-General Kofi Annan.","wpu":"Raoul Wallenberg Monument, Gothenburg","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Raoul_Wallenberg_minnesmarke_-_C_Gyllenhammar.jpg/960px-Raoul_Wallenberg_minnesmarke_-_C_Gyllenhammar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Masthugg Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.697222,"lo":11.9375,"d":853,"ds":"Masthugg Church (Swedish: Masthuggskyrkan) in Gothenburg, Sweden, was built in 1914. Its position on a high hill (Masthugget) close to the city and near the Göta älv makes it a striking sight – the church tower is 60 m (200 ft) high in itself. The church represents the National Romantic style in Nordic architecture and was designed by Sigfrid Ericson.","wpu":"Masthugg Church","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/Masthugget_Church_KDH_070405.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Lindholmen Science Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.706944,"lo":11.94,"d":888,"ds":"Lindholmen Science Park is a science park dedicated to research and development in mobile communication, intelligent vehicles and transports systems, and modern media industry, located in Lindholmen Gothenburg, Sweden. Chalmers University of Technology, the University of Gothenburg, and the IT University of Göteborg collaborate with high tech industries and the local community in different development projects.","wpu":"Lindholmen Science Park","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/45/Lindholmen_Science_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Backa Theatre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.705226,"lo":11.935304,"d":991,"ds":"Backa Theatre (Swedish: Backa Teater) is a youth theater located in Gothenburg, Sweden. It focuses on producing theater for children and their families. The theater (then known as Skolteatern) was founded in 1978 by Eva Bergman, Ulf Dohlsten, and Maria Hedborg as part of Gothenburg City Theatre.","wpu":"Backa Theatre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Backa_Teater%2C_Gothenburg_%28rear%29.JPG/960px-Backa_Teater%2C_Gothenburg_%28rear%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kungsparken, Gothenburg","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":57.701039,"lo":11.967458,"d":1037,"ds":"Kungsparken (Swedish for King's park), originally Kungsportsparken, is a 13-hectare park behind Stora Teatern in Gothenburg, which encloses most of Nya Allén walkway, and constitutes a so-called park district. Kungsparken was built in several stages between 1839 and 1861. In the 1940s and 1950s, the park was a meeting place for the city's homosexual men.","wpu":"Kungsparken, Gothenburg","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Fotografi%2C_photography-Anna_Backlund_-_G%C3%B6teborgs_stadsmuseum_-_GhmPK_2497.tif/lossy-page1-960px-Fotografi%2C_photography-Anna_Backlund_-_G%C3%B6teborgs_stadsmuseum_-_GhmPK_2497.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Gothenburg ( GOTH-ən-burg; Swedish: Göteborg [jœtəˈbɔrj] ) is the second-largest city in Sweden, after the capital Stockholm, and the fifth-largest in the Nordic countries. Situated by the Kattegat on the west coast of Sweden 400 km from the capital, it is the gubernatorial seat of Västra Götaland County, with a population of approximately 600,000 in the city proper and about 1.1 million inhabitants in the metropolitan area. King Gustavus Adolphus founded Gothenburg by royal charter in 1621 as a heavily fortified, primarily Dutch, trading colony.","wpu":"Gothenburg","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/G%C3%B6teborg_2503_stitch_%2828573994096%29.jpg/960px-G%C3%B6teborg_2503_stitch_%2828573994096%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-26"}